"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][19]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M19-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M19-L1 – Deep Architectures and Gradient Flow<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M19 – Advanced Deep Learning<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M14, M16, M9.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Analyse products of gradients\nand use initialisation, normalisation and residual paths with\njustification.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Depth as a computational problem<\/h2>\n<p>A network with many layers can first derive short patterns from a\nsensor signal, then periodic structures and ultimately the states of a\nplant. However, extra layers are useful only if training reaches their\nparameters. Write\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mi>l<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_l=\\phi_l(W_lh_{l-1}+b_l)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mi>l<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J_l=\\partial h_l/\\partial h_{l-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUsing column vectors, the chain rule gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/msub><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>J<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msubsup><msubsup><mi>J<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>⋯<\/mi><msubsup><mi>J<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><\/msub><mi>L<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_{h_0}L=J_1^\\top J_2^\\top\\cdots J_d^\\top\\nabla_{h_d}L.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>An upper bound is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><\/msub><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><msub><mo>∏<\/mo><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><msub><mi>J<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\|\\nabla_{h_d}L\\|\\prod_l\\|J_l\\|_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNot every direction vector attains this bound. Repeated attenuation can\nmake the gradient practically vanish; repeated amplification can render\nupdates unusable. Saturating activations often exacerbate the first\nproblem.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A scalar experiment<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001589_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Logarithmic plot of repeated factors 0.8, 1.0 and 1.2 over 30 layers.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Logarithmic plot of repeated factors 0.8,\n1.0 and 1.2 over 30 layers.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Gradient flow across network depth.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Consider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mi>l<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_l=a h_{l-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_0=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=h_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith 30 layers and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe derivative is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>0.8<\/mn><mn>30<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.001238<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.8^{30}\\approx0.001238<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=1.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>237.38<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">237.38<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe teaching diagram plots the magnitudes on a logarithmic axis. In a\nresidual block\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mi>l<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_{l+1}=h_l+F_l(h_l)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe local Jacobian is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>J<\/mi><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I+J_{F_l}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe direct path improves the prospects for gradient flow but does not\nguarantee a stable overall gradient.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>d<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.arange(<span class=\"dv\">31<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.0012379400392853823<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(<span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">237.37631379976955<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Initialisation and normalisation<\/h2>\n<p>Under simplifying independence assumptions, a weighted signal with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninputs and centred weights of variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a second moment proportional to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">nv<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA ReLU halves this moment in expectation for symmetric preactivations.\nHe initialisation therefore often uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=2/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor ReLU, giving a standard deviation of 0.125 for 128 inputs. Glorot\ninitialisation accounts for input and output dimensions\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2/(n_{in}+n_{out})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nBoth are starting conditions, not guarantees during training. Batch\nnormalisation uses batch statistics and requires appropriate inference\nstatistics; layer normalisation operates per example across selected\nfeature axes. With very small batches the choice can be decisive.<\/p>\n<p>In practice, log activation moments, gradient norms per block,\nupdate-to-weight ratios and validation loss. Clipping may help with\nexploding gradients; when gradients systematically vanish it cannot\nreplace an architectural correction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Two scalar residual blocks have local derivatives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat is the overall derivative? Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.88<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.1\\cdot0.8=0.88<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith ten identical factors of 0.88 it would be about 0.279. Even\nresidual paths can attenuate signals over many blocks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://d2l.ai/chapter_multilayer-perceptrons/numerical-stability-and-init.html\">Dive\ninto Deep Learning, 5.4: Numerical Stability and Initialization<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\ninitialisation and gradient norms<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.deeplearningbook.org/contents/optimization.html\">Goodfellow,\nBengio, Courville: Deep Learning, Chapter 8<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · optimisation problems,\ninitialisation, adaptive methods<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.03385\">He et al. (2015): Deep\nResidual Learning for Image Recognition<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · residual connections<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M19-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M19-L2 – CNNs, ResNets and Efficient Convolution<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M19 – Advanced Deep Learning<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M14, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M19-L1<\/a>,\nM2.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine output shapes,\nparameter counts, receptive fields and computational costs of\nconvolutional blocks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Local weights instead of dense connections<\/h2>\n<p>In an image of a circuit board, scratches are to be detected\nirrespective of their position. A CNN uses shared local filters for this\npurpose. The operation commonly used in libraries is cross-correlation\n(without flipping the kernel). For a square kernel of size\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\npadding\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nstride\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand dilation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe output height is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">⌊<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>p<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>s<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">⌋<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H&#39;=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{H+2p-d(k-1)-1}{s}\\right\\rfloor+1.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The width is analogous. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{in}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninput channels and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{out}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noutput channels, dense convolution contains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k^2 C_{in}C_{out}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nweights plus optionally\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{out}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbias values. Translation equivariance holds under idealised conditions\nfor unit strides and away from boundary artefacts; pooling and larger\nstrides alter this property.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From filter to residual block<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001573_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Dense convolution uses 4,608 weights; depthwise convolution plus 1-by-1 mixing uses 656.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Dense convolution uses 4,608 weights;\ndepthwise convolution plus 1-by-1 mixing uses 656.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Dense and depthwise-separable convolution.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A 32×32-pixel image with 16 channels is processed with 32 filters of\nsize 3×3, stride 1 and padding 1. The output is 32×32×32. The 4,608\nweights entail 4,718,592 multiply-accumulate operations (MACs, excluding\nbias) per image. A separable variant with one filter per channel\nfollowed by 1×1 mixing has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>32<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>656<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9\\cdot16+16\\cdot32=656<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nweights and 671,744 MACs. This is not an identical parametrisation, but\nit can reduce costs if performance is acceptable. The diagram compares\nthe two paths. Actual latency also depends on memory access and kernel\nimplementation.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>H,Cin,Cout,k<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">32<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">16<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">32<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>full<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>Cin<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>Cout<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>sep<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>Cin<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>Cin<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>Cout<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> (full,sep,H<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>H<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>full,H<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>H<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>sep)<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">4608<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">656<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4718592<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">671744<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(full<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>sep)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Receptive fields, residuals and practical architecture<\/h2>\n<p>The theoretical receptive field can be tracked with jump\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j_0=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand field size\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_0=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi>l<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mrow><mi>l<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_l=r_{l-1}+(k_l-1)d_lj_{l-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mrow><mi>l<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j_l=j_{l-1}s_l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTwo 3×3 layers with stride 1 reach a 5×5 field; with stride 2 in the\nfirst layer they reach a 7×7 field. The effective field may be smaller.\nResidual blocks add a transformation to the input; if channel counts or\nspatial dimensions differ, an appropriate 1×1 projection or downsampling\ncan be used. Grouped convolution limits channel interactions; 1×1\nconvolution mixes channels without a spatial neighbourhood. In\nsegmentation, loss of resolution and small objects should be explicitly\nevaluated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>A 64×64 input with eight channels is processed with 16 filters,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSolution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>32<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H&#39;=32<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nweights\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>25<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">25\\cdot8\\cdot16=3200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nMACs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>32<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3200<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>276<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>800<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">32^2\\cdot3200=3{,}276{,}800<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBias values would be added separately. A second 3×3 layer with stride 1\nreaches a 9×9 field.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://d2l.ai/chapter_convolutional-neural-networks/conv-layer.html\">Dive\ninto Deep Learning, 7.2: Convolutions for Images<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · cross-correlation\nand convolution<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.03385\">He et al. (2015): Deep\nResidual Learning for Image Recognition<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · residual connections<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M19-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M19-L3 – Recurrent Networks, LSTM and Sequence Modelling<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M19 – Advanced Deep Learning<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M14, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M19-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L9&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M18-L9<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate time-dependent states\nand LSTM gates and distinguish sequence training from inference.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>State and unfolding through time<\/h2>\n<p>A measuring device provides a temperature every second. The current\nmeasurement alone is insufficient to detect a gradual trend. A recurrent\nnetwork computes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tanh<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_t=\\tanh(W_xx_t+W_hh_{t-1}+b)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe same parameters are reused along the time axis. In backpropagation\nthrough time (BPTT), parameter gradients from multiple time steps are\nsummed; long products of state derivatives can become unstable.\nTruncated BPTT limits the backward path but can make long-range\ndependencies harder to learn.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>LSTM: controlled memory<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001632_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Contributions from initial memory decay as new contributions accumulate; the total approaches 0.8.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Contributions from initial memory decay\nas new contributions accumulate; the total approaches 0.8.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Memory with constant LSTM gates.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>;<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_t=[x_t;h_{t-1}]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe forget, input and output gates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>o<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_t,i_t,o_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare each computed by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma(Wu_t+b)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the candidate for writing is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tanh<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_t=\\tanh(W_gu_t+b_g)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nElementwise multiplication is denoted by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>⊙<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\odot<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>⊙<\/mo><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>⊙<\/mo><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>o<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>⊙<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tanh<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_t=f_t\\odot c_{t-1}+i_t\\odot g_t,\\qquad h_t=o_t\\odot\\tanh(c_t).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For a scalar memory cell with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_0=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_1=1.7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.748<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_1\\approx0.748<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the gates remain unchanged, the cell state approaches 0.8; the\nteaching diagram shows the initial contribution and the newly written\nportion. The direct partial derivative path through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe full derivative also includes the dependence of the gates on earlier\nstates.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>c<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> cs<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[c]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> t <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    c<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.75<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>c<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    cs.append(c)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(cs[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],<span class=\"fl\">1.7<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(cs[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.75<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.tanh(cs[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Training and use in sequences<\/h2>\n<p>With variable lengths, padding positions are masked in the loss;\nstates must not inadvertently be passed between independent devices.\nTemporal separation of training and test data is particularly important\nfor forecasting. A bidirectional network can use context before and\nafter a position in completed recordings, but is inappropriate for\nreal-time prediction without future observations. For autoregressive\noutput, teacher forcing uses known earlier target values during\ntraining; at inference only values generated so far are available. This\ndifference can allow errors to accumulate. GRUs are an alternative\narchitecture with fewer explicit gate components.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>An input pulse leaves\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_0=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nafter which nothing more is written and the gate remains at 0.9. After\nten steps,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>0.9<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.3487<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.9^{10}\\approx0.3487<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains. The half-life in steps is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>6.58<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log(0.5)/\\log(0.9)\\approx6.58<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is a model with a fixed gate, not a fixed memory length of a\ntrained LSTM.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://d2l.ai/chapter_recurrent-modern/lstm.html\">Dive\ninto Deep Learning, 10.1: Long Short-Term Memory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · LSTM gates and\nstates<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.deeplearningbook.org/contents/optimization.html\">Goodfellow,\nBengio, Courville: Deep Learning, Chapter 8<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · optimisation problems,\ninitialisation, adaptive methods<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M19-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M19-L4 – Attention and Transformer Architecture<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M19 – Advanced Deep Learning<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M14, M2, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M19-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate scaled dot-product\nattention including masking and identify Transformer components.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Addressing rather than merely propagating state<\/h2>\n<p>In a sequence of sensor messages, a later error message may refer to\na restart far in the past. Attention allows each position to weight\ninformation from other positions according to its features. From an\ninput matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times d}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntrainable projections produce the query\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=XW_Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nkey\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>K<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=XW_K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=XW_V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOne head computes<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">softmax<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">row<\/mtext><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><msup><mi>K<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><mo>+<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\operatorname{softmax}_{\\text{row}}(QK^\\top/\\sqrt{d_k}+M),\\qquad Z=AV.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontains 0 for permitted positions and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor forbidden positions. Scaling by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{d_k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkeeps the variance of dot products manageable under simplifying\nassumptions. Every row of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthat is not fully masked sums to one. A fully masked row requires\nspecial handling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example with three positions<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001511_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Matrix of allowed attention weights with future positions masked.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Matrix of allowed attention weights with\nfuture positions masked.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Causal attention matrix.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=K=((1,0),(0,1),(1,1))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand scalar values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,4,8)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the first position the unmasked scores are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,0,1)/\\sqrt{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe weights are approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.401<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.198<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.401<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.401,0.198,0.401)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the output is about 4.802. With a causal mask the first position may\nread only its own information: the result is 2. The third position may\nuse all three values. The diagram shows the causal weight matrix;\ncrossed-out cells contribute nothing.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>Q<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>],[<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>],[<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>V<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">8.<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> scores<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>Q<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>Q.T<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>np.sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> softmax(s):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    e<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.exp(s<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>s.<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,keepdims<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>))<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> e<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>e.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,keepdims<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>A<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>softmax(scores)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>B<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>softmax(np.where(np.triu(np.ones((<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>),<span class=\"bu\">bool<\/span>),<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>),<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>np.inf,scores))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(B.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>),<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose((B<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>V)[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(A[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],A<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>V,B<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>V)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A complete block<\/h2>\n<p>Multi-head attention combines several projections and mixes the\nresults again. A position-wise feedforward network transforms the\nfeatures nonlinearly. Residual connections and normalisation support\noptimisation. Positional information, such as added encodings or\nrelative/rotary positional mechanisms, distinguishes order. Encoders\ntypically use bidirectional self-attention, while decoders for\nautoregressive language use a causal mask. In encoder-decoder models,\ncross-attention connects decoder queries to encoder keys and values.\nDense attention requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n^2d_k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noperations per head to calculate scores; an explicit score matrix uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmemory. Memory-optimised algorithms can avoid materialising the matrix.\nAttention weights alone are not a reliable explanation of a model\ndecision.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>With 2,048 positions and eight heads, how many score elements are\nproduced per layer in a dense implementation? Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>2048<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>33<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>554<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>432<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\cdot2048^2=33{,}554{,}432<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt two bytes per element this is 64 MiB, excluding gradients and other\ntensors. A causal mask does not automatically avoid quadratic\nallocation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762\">Vaswani et al. (2017):\nAttention Is All You Need<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Transformer and attention<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M19-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M19-L5 – Transfer Learning and Self-Supervised Learning<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M19 – Advanced Deep Learning<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M14, M10, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M19-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compare pretrained models,\nself-generated targets and appropriate adaptation strategies.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Which information can be transferred?<\/h2>\n<p>A company has many unlabelled photos of components, but only a few\nwith verified defect classes. Instead of training a large model from\nrandom weights, it can adopt a pretrained encoder\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith a frozen encoder, only the head\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>ψ<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_\\psi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis adjusted. Fine-tuning also changes encoder parameters. Partial\nadaptation trains selected blocks; parameter-efficient methods add small\ntrainable components. No strategy guarantees transfer: differences in\nsensors, materials, perspectives and defect types can invalidate the\nrepresentation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-supervision as the creation of learning tasks<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001727_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Diagram connecting general pretraining and target-task adaptation.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Diagram connecting general pretraining\nand target-task adaptation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Pretraining and transfer to a target task.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Unlabelled data provide their own training signals: masked token\nprediction hides parts and reconstructs them from context;\nautoregressive tasks predict the next element; image methods can\nestimate missing regions. For masked positions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℳ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfor example,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℳ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℳ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=-\\frac1{|\\mathcal M|}\\sum_{t\\in\\mathcal M}\\log p_\\theta(x_t\\mid\\widetilde x).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widetilde x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the modified input, not the unobscured original content. With two\ncorrect-target probabilities of 0.8 and 0.25, the mean cross-entropy is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.805<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\log(0.2)/2\\approx0.805<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram shows an original three-stage procedure: a learning signal\nfrom raw data, encoder pretraining, and separate adaptation and\nevaluation. Self-supervision avoids manually assigned target classes for\nthis task, not necessarily data preparation or checking rights.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Costs and comparison<\/h2>\n<p>An encoder with 8 million parameters produces vectors of length 512;\nthe new head distinguishes five classes. Its number of weights and bias\nvalues is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>512<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2565<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">512\\cdot5+5=2565<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOnly 0.0321% of the total parameters are trainable when the encoder is\nfrozen, but encoder runtime remains part of inference. With a low-rank\nupdate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta W=BA<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a 512×512 matrix of rank at most 8 for the update, there are 8,192\nadditional weights instead of 262,144 fully trainable weights. The\noriginal matrix remains stored.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>head<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">512<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> enc<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">8_000_000<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> head<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2565<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">512<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">8<\/span><span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">8192<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>np.log([<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.25<\/span>]).mean(),<span class=\"fl\">.8047189562170501<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>head<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(enc<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>head))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Compare random initialisation, a frozen encoder and fine-tuning using\nthe same target training and test data; document external pretraining\ndata as far as known. When labels are scarce, validation groups should\nbe separated by device or recording time. For reproducible features, a\nfrozen encoder may also need to be placed in evaluation mode so that\ndropout/batch normalisation do not cause unwanted changes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>With three target classes and a feature width of 128, a head needs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>128<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>387<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">128\\cdot3+3=387<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nparameters. A rank-4 update to a 128×256 matrix needs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>128<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>256<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1536<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4(128+256)=1536<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nparameters, excluding bias. Decide on the basis of target performance,\ncomputational budget and shifts in the data distribution, not parameter\nsavings alone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/transfer_learning_tutorial.html\">PyTorch:\nTransfer Learning for Computer Vision Tutorial<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · freezing and\nfine-tuning<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.04805\">Devlin et al. (2018):\nBERT<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pretraining and transfer<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M19-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M19-L6 – Representation Learning and Contrastive Objectives<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M19 – Advanced Deep Learning<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12, M14, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M19-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate similarity-based\nlearning objectives and select meaningful positive and negative\nexamples.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From raw data to a geometry<\/h2>\n<p>A warehouse management system is to find similar product views\nwithout class labels. An encoder maps different crops of the same object\nto vectors. For normalised vectors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncosine similarity is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{ij}=z_i^\\top z_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn contrastive learning, another crop of the same image is selected as a\npositive pair, for example; other objects form negative pairs. Good\naugmentation preserves the properties important for the later task. If\nthe colour of a component is changed even though it determines its type\nclass, the learned invariance may be harmful.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A contrastive loss<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001611_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Unit-circle diagram showing the reference vector and positive and negative examples with their similarities.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Unit-circle diagram showing the reference\nvector and positive and negative examples with their\nsimilarities.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Contrastive vector geometry.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For an anchor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand its positive partner\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>j<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒞<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal C_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the candidate set excluding the anchor but including\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>j<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe temperature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nscales the similarities:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒞<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell_{i,j}=-\\log\\frac{\\exp(s_{ij}/\\tau)}{\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal C_i}\\exp(s_{ik}/\\tau)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_i=(1,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_j=(0.8,0.6)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand two negatives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe similarities are 0.8, 0 and -1. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe logits are 1.6, 0 and -2, the probability of the positive pair is\nabout 0.814 and the loss about 0.206. The original diagram shows the\nfour vectors on the unit circle. A low temperature sharpens the ranking\nbut can be particularly sensitive to false negatives.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>z<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>],[<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span>],[<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>],[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(np.linalg.norm(z,axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>),<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>s<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>z[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:]<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>z[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>loss<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.logaddexp.<span class=\"bu\">reduce<\/span>(s)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>s[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">float<\/span>(np.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>loss)),<span class=\"bu\">float<\/span>(loss))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.80<\/span><span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span>np.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>loss)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.83<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning strategy and evaluation<\/h2>\n<p>Using two augmentations per input, both views can be used in turn as\nanchors. Other examples of the same actual type may mistakenly be\ntreated as negative pairs. A projection head after the encoder enables\noptimisation of the training objective in a separate space; the\npreceding encoder features may be better suited to later use.\nAlternatives without explicit negative pairs need other mechanisms\nagainst collapse, such as asymmetric target paths or variance\nconstraints. Test representations with a frozen encoder and a linear\nclassifier, nearest-neighbour search and transfer to new devices. Also\ncheck that the vectors do not all collapse to the same point; high\ntraining similarity alone is insufficient.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>With three candidates of equal similarity, the positive share is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nregardless of temperature; the loss is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.099<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log 3\\approx1.099<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCan the loss alone show that all target classes are separated? No. It\nevaluates the chosen pair relationships; their correspondence with later\nclasses must be assessed separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.05709\">Chen et al. (2020): A\nSimple Framework for Contrastive Learning of Visual Representations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· contrastive learning and augmentation<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M19-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M19-L7 – Optimisation of Large Models and Scaling<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M19 – Advanced Deep Learning<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M9, M14, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M19-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Roughly estimate a training\nbudget and distinguish memory requirements, parallelisation and\noptimisation methods.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Compute budget and model size<\/h2>\n<p>A language-based document system is to be pretrained with a fixed\nbudget. More parameters without sufficient training data are not\nautomatically advantageous. For a dense autoregressive Transformer, the\nrough training estimate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>N<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\approx6ND<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nFLOPs is often used, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntrainable parameters and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nprocessed tokens. The factor approximates forward and backward\ncomputation for dense matrices; attention with long sequences,\nembeddings, extra recomputation and hardware utilisation can alter the\nactual calculation.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncounts processed tokens, not necessarily distinct tokens. Empirical\nscaling laws depend on data, architecture, target quantity and tested\nranges.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A budget calculation<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001703_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Comparison of model size and training-data allocation under limited computational resources.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Comparison of model size and\ntraining-data allocation under limited computational\nresources.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Scaling under a fixed compute budget.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>9<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=10^9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>9<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=20\\cdot10^9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe approximation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>20<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.2\\cdot10^{20}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nFLOPs. If a system effectively delivers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>60<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>12<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60\\cdot10^{12}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nFLOP/s, an idealised\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\cdot10^6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nseconds or about 23.15 days are required. Doubling the parameter count\nat the same budget halves the possible token count. The diagram presents\nthis budget curve. It is not an empirical law of performance and\ncontains no prices.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>N,D<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">9<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>C<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>N<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>D<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>seconds<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>C<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">60<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> C<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">120_000_000_000_000_000_000<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> seconds<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2_000_000<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(seconds<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">86400<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Memory and distributed updates<\/h2>\n<p>An illustrative mixed-precision Adam state contains, per parameter,\ntwo bytes for the model weight, two bytes for the gradient, four bytes\nfor the master weight and four bytes for each of two moment estimators:\n16 bytes in total. For one billion parameters this is 16 GB (decimal),\nexcluding activations, temporary tensors and communication buffers;\nimplementations vary. Gradient accumulation processes multiple\nmicrobatches before an update; with equal weighting and without\nbatch-dependent operations, their mean gradient corresponds to that of a\nlarger batch. Activation checkpointing trades memory for recomputation.\nData parallelism replicates weights and aggregates gradients; tensor\nparallelism divides matrix operations, pipeline parallelism divides\nlayers, and sharding distributes states and requires communication. At\nlow precision, loss scaling can reduce gradient underflow; instabilities\nmust be detected. AdamW decouples the weight shrinkage term from the\nadaptively scaled data-loss gradient. Warm-up, learning-rate schedules\nand clipping should be investigated in a controlled manner.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Four processes each handle eight examples in six accumulation steps.\nOne update sees 192 examples (with complete, equally sized batches). If\n10,000 tokens are processed per process and microbatch, there are\n240,000 tokens per update. A model twice as large costs approximately\ntwice as much for the same token count, but does not necessarily require\ntwice the wall-clock time.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.deeplearningbook.org/contents/optimization.html\">Goodfellow,\nBengio, Courville: Deep Learning, Chapter 8<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · optimisation problems,\ninitialisation, adaptive methods<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.15556\">Hoffmann et al. (2022):\nTraining Compute-Optimal Large Language Models<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · compute budget,\ndata and model size<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.05101\">Loshchilov and Hutter\n(2017): Decoupled Weight Decay Regularization<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · decoupled weight\ndecay<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M19-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M19-L8 – Experiments, Ablation and Training Stability<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M19 – Advanced Deep Learning<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6, M10, M15, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M19-L7<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Evaluate controlled ablations\nwith multiple repetitions and diagnose instability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>One change rather than many claims<\/h2>\n<p>A team develops a model to classify synthetic machine signals. The\nnew version contains residual paths and an augmentation. Greater\nperformance of the combined version does not prove which component was\nhelpful. A factorial design with each of the two components on/off gives\nfour combinations. Use the same data split, training budget, search\nbudget and metric; pair random initialisations where possible. A\ncomparison specified in advance protects against retrospectively\nselecting the most favourable result. The test set is used at the end of\nthe predetermined decision process, not for repeated optimisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Ablation with synthetic measurements<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001502_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Comparison of experimental configurations over three random seeds.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Comparison of experimental configurations\nover three random seeds.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Ablation results across three seeds.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The following table contains explicitly invented validation values\n(accuracy in per cent), not measured model performance.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Seed<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Baseline<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Residual only<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Augmentation only<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Both<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>11<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">80<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">83<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">81<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">86<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>22<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">82<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">84<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">84<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">87<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>33<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">81<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">85<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">82<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">88<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The mean residual effect without augmentation is 3 points, and with\naugmentation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>14<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">14/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npoints. The interaction effect is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npoints. The two components together yield paired improvements of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(6,5,7)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npoints, with mean 6 and sample standard deviation 1. The figure shows\nthe individual paired results; it does not illustrate experimentally\nobserved performance. A t-interval with three independent and\napproximately normally distributed seed differences is very wide:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mn>4.303<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\pm4.303/\\sqrt3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor approximately [3.52; 8.48]. It does not capture uncertainty due to\nnew test populations.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.stats <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> t<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"dv\">80<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">83<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">81<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">86<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">82<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">84<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">84<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">87<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">81<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">85<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">82<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">88<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>d<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>x[:,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x[:,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>interaction<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>x[:,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x[:,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x[:,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>x[:,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(interaction.mean(),<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> d.mean()<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>ci<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>d.mean()<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>t.ppf(<span class=\"fl\">.975<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>d.std(ddof<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>np.sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(ci)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Diagnosis before conclusions<\/h2>\n<p>Log loss and validation metrics against the same number of processed\nexamples or tokens; also record wall-clock time, learning rate, gradient\nnorms, outliers, data-loading errors and memory. With NaN/Inf, check\ndata, logarithms and divisions first, then precision and updates. An\nexcessively large learning-rate increase can cause loss spikes;\npersistently poor validation with low training loss instead suggests\ngeneralisation problems. Do not selectively repeat failed runs until\nthey succeed. Document seeds, libraries, model versions, data hashes and\nselection rules. For many comparisons, use appropriate multiple-testing\nmethods or mark the analysis as exploratory.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>An additional seed produces -2 points for the combined version\nrelative to the baseline. The new mean difference is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(6+5+7-2)/4=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe result is not more reliable merely because there are more runs if\nselection rules were violated; the correct approach is to report the\nplanned run fully and recalculate the dispersion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.deeplearningbook.org/contents/optimization.html\">Goodfellow,\nBengio, Courville: Deep Learning, Chapter 8<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · optimisation problems,\ninitialisation, adaptive methods<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.05709\">Chen et al. (2020): A\nSimple Framework for Contrastive Learning of Visual Representations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· contrastive learning and augmentation<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M19-L9":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L9\">M19-L9 – Non-Convex Optimisation and Landscapes of Deep Networks<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M19 – Advanced Deep Learning<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Elective specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M9, M17, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M19-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M19-L8<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Recognise saddle points,\nsymmetries and local curvature and explain the limitations of\ntwo-dimensional loss plots.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stationary does not mean optimal<\/h2>\n<p>In a deep network, training loss as a function of all weights is\ngenerally non-convex. A stationary point satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla L=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbut can be a minimum, maximum or saddle point. For twice differentiable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Hessian examines local curvature. Mixed positive and negative\neigenvalues at a stationary point establish a saddle. Positive\ndefiniteness is sufficient for a strict local minimum, but\nsemidefiniteness alone is not. At ReLU kinks, an appropriate local\nanalysis must replace the classical second derivative.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A teaching model with two valleys<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001732_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Contours showing two local minima and a saddle point between them.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Contours showing two local minima and a\nsaddle point between them.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Two minima and a saddle point.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>We examine the following original two-dimensional function:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L(u,v)=(u^2-1)^2+v^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The gradient is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4u(u^2-1),2v)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the Hessian is diagonal with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(12u^2-4,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe stationary points are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\pm1,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt the origin the eigenvalues are -4 and 2, hence a saddle; at the other\ntwo points they are 8 and 2, hence strict minima of value 0. The diagram\nshows contour lines and marks all three points. Gradient descent with a\nlearning rate of 0.05 from the starting point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.1,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmoves towards the right-hand valley; deterministic descent starting\nexactly on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nby contrast, does not leave this axis of symmetry.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> grad(w):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    u,v<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>w<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>u<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(u<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>u<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>),<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>v])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>w<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">150<\/span>): w<span class=\"op\">-=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.05<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>grad(w)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(w,[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],atol<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-6<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(grad([<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]),<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(w)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Significance and limitations for deep networks<\/h2>\n<p>Swapping neurons in a hidden layer can leave the network function\nunchanged if the following weights are swapped correspondingly. Scaling\nsymmetries also often produce equivalent parametrisations: for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ReLU<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ReLU<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{ReLU}(ax)=a\\operatorname{ReLU}(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nholds, so preceding and subsequent weights can be scaled in opposite\ndirections. A raw curvature measure therefore depends on\nparametrisation. A visualisation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L(\\theta_0+u d_1+v d_2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nshows only the chosen plane of the high-dimensional landscape.\nDirections should be meaningfully normalised and results checked across\nseveral slices. Wide valleys are not automatically evidence of good\ngeneralisation. Stochastic gradients, momentum and suitable\ninitialisation can alter the trajectory; a general proof of global\noptimality does not follow. Validation remains necessary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Examine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L(u,v)=u^4+v^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the origin. The Hessian eigenvalues are 0 and 2, yet the point is a\nstrict global minimum because both terms are non-negative and vanish\ntogether only there. This explains why vanishing curvature does not\nautomatically imply a flat set of minima or a saddle.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.deeplearningbook.org/contents/optimization.html\">Goodfellow,\nBengio, Courville: Deep Learning, Chapter 8<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · optimisation problems,\ninitialisation, adaptive methods<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.09913\">Li et al. (2018):\nVisualizing the Loss Landscape of Neural Nets<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · visualisation limits\nand normalisation<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
